00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:52.380
on how we deploy Kubernetes,
how we manage it.

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:54.880
Tina's going to talk to you
about how SRE keeps clusters

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:57.730
healthy, and what I want to do
is share some best practices

00:00:57.730 --> 00:00:59.290
in taking an
application and making

00:00:59.290 --> 00:01:00.940
it resilient to zonal failure.

00:01:05.010 --> 00:01:08.660
So let's start with the K. If
you have been around GCP Next

00:01:08.660 --> 00:01:11.880
this week, you've probably heard
this word a few hundred times

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:14.200
now, so this slide may
be a little irrelevant,

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:17.280
but Kubernetes is an open
source container orchestration

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:19.530
framework, originally
built by Google,

00:01:19.530 --> 00:01:21.505
and now in the open
source community.

00:01:21.505 --> 00:01:24.030
It is based off our
experience running containers

00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:25.682
at scale with Borg.

00:01:25.682 --> 00:01:28.400
Kubernetes can run on
any infrastructure.

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:31.200
It can run in the cloud
as a hosted offering.

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:34.110
It could run on-prem.

00:01:34.110 --> 00:01:36.570
But the important thing
to know for today's talk

00:01:36.570 --> 00:01:39.930
is that Kubernetes is all
about managing applications,

00:01:39.930 --> 00:01:41.383
not infrastructure.

00:01:45.330 --> 00:01:48.660
GKE is, of course,
Google Kubernetes engine.

00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:50.970
It's a mature product
that has been generally

00:01:50.970 --> 00:01:53.547
available for three years
now, although to be honest,

00:01:53.547 --> 00:01:55.380
I think it's three years
and, like, a month,

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:59.370
but to the significant digit,
I think that's pretty good.

00:01:59.370 --> 00:02:02.190
If you're interested
in following GKE--

00:02:02.190 --> 00:02:05.070
our updates, tips, and tricks,
I recommend checking out

00:02:05.070 --> 00:02:07.810
the Google Cloud Platform blog.

00:02:07.810 --> 00:02:09.810
So with a little bit of
that product background,

00:02:09.810 --> 00:02:13.590
Tina is going to talk
to you more about GCP.

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:15.240
TINA ZHANG: Thanks, Jeff.

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:18.150
So let's say you've
decided that GKE

00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:20.880
is the right solution
for your workloads,

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:24.660
a next key decision that will
influence the reliability

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:27.030
and availability
of your application

00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:31.880
is how you deploy your GKE
cluster across GCP locations.

00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:33.930
So at the moment
you have a choice

00:02:33.930 --> 00:02:37.980
of 17 regions and
52 zones, and these

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:40.440
are expanding all the time.

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:45.180
So regions are independent
geographic areas, or loosely

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:50.220
speaking, a campus of data
centers that consist of zones,

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:55.020
and a zone is a deployment area
for cloud platform resources

00:02:55.020 --> 00:02:56.260
within a region.

00:02:56.260 --> 00:02:58.980
So between zones
of the same region

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:03.240
you have high-bandwidth,
low-latency network connections

00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:07.380
that you can typically expect
less than the millisecond,

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:09.660
in the 95th percentile.

00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:12.640
And from an availability
point of view,

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:17.010
zones should be considered
a single point of failure--

00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:20.010
the points of failure
domain within a region.

00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:23.580
Let's take a look at
how that works in GKE.

00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:27.930
So the most basic deployment
is what we call a zonal cluster

00:03:27.930 --> 00:03:29.730
deployment, like
the one pictured

00:03:29.730 --> 00:03:33.330
here, where you have a single
master along with nodes,

00:03:33.330 --> 00:03:35.220
all in the same zone.

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:38.400
And when you run a
Kubernetes cluster on GKE,

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:43.560
we host the master VM in
our own Google project,

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:46.720
and you create work
nodes in your project.

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:49.260
So in this mode
Kubernetes will mitigate

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:51.690
against individual
machine failures,

00:03:51.690 --> 00:03:55.980
but in the event of a zonal
failure such as an asteroid

00:03:55.980 --> 00:04:00.220
striking a data center, the
entire cluster will go down.

00:04:00.220 --> 00:04:03.990
So in order to deploy
fault-tolerant applications

00:04:03.990 --> 00:04:07.770
with high availability, you
should deploy your applications

00:04:07.770 --> 00:04:09.900
across multiple
zones in a region

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:13.780
in order to help protect against
this single point of failure,

00:04:13.780 --> 00:04:19.380
and that's what we call a
multi-zonal node deployment.

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:23.410
So in this configuration GKE
will distribute your work nodes

00:04:23.410 --> 00:04:26.050
across multiple zones
within the same region,

00:04:26.050 --> 00:04:29.800
though you have to have at
least one node in the same zone

00:04:29.800 --> 00:04:34.300
as your master, and you cannot
deploy nodes in a completely

00:04:34.300 --> 00:04:36.230
separate region.

00:04:36.230 --> 00:04:38.650
And if you are using
cluster auto-scaler

00:04:38.650 --> 00:04:41.050
and your cluster
contains node pools that

00:04:41.050 --> 00:04:44.280
have the same instance
type, cluster scaler

00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:48.070
will try to keep those node
pool sizes balanced for you

00:04:48.070 --> 00:04:50.380
when scaling up in
order to prevent

00:04:50.380 --> 00:04:53.200
against uneven distribution
of node pool sizes

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:55.280
across multiple zones.

00:04:55.280 --> 00:04:58.840
So this means that an asteroid
strike in a single zone

00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:01.339
won't take down
your entire cluster.

00:05:01.339 --> 00:05:03.130
And Jeff is going to
describe those failure

00:05:03.130 --> 00:05:04.441
modes in more detail.

00:05:08.680 --> 00:05:10.870
JEFF JOHNSON: So let's look
at a typical application

00:05:10.870 --> 00:05:13.210
deployed on GKE.

00:05:13.210 --> 00:05:14.910
So if we look at
this diagram, we

00:05:14.910 --> 00:05:18.110
have that hosted master
node, in US-central1-f,

00:05:18.110 --> 00:05:20.830
and that's in the Google Manage
project, so its actually a bit

00:05:20.830 --> 00:05:22.900
opaque to you as a
user of GKE, but its

00:05:22.900 --> 00:05:25.460
important to know what's there.

00:05:25.460 --> 00:05:27.950
And we have our nodes
across three zones,

00:05:27.950 --> 00:05:32.770
so each one of those green
boxes maps to a GCE VM.

00:05:32.770 --> 00:05:35.020
These little yellow
checkered boxes above,

00:05:35.020 --> 00:05:37.060
that's representing your
application workload.

00:05:37.060 --> 00:05:38.980
So in this case, we
have a deployment,

00:05:38.980 --> 00:05:42.640
we have asked them for three
replicas of that deployment,

00:05:42.640 --> 00:05:47.530
and Kubernetes has chosen to
spread them across these zones.

00:05:47.530 --> 00:05:50.550
So what happens when
that asteroid strikes?

00:05:50.550 --> 00:05:51.990
So we've lost our VMs.

00:05:51.990 --> 00:05:54.550
They are offline,
cannot be reached.

00:05:54.550 --> 00:05:57.900
But we've maintained the
other set of machines.

00:05:57.900 --> 00:06:01.080
The other two failure domains
there are happy and healthy,

00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:04.547
so they're able to
continue to serve traffic.

00:06:04.547 --> 00:06:06.380
Instances that are
running on those machines

00:06:06.380 --> 00:06:08.360
are going to be unaffected,
by the Kubernetes

00:06:08.360 --> 00:06:10.490
master is regularly
pooling and looking

00:06:10.490 --> 00:06:13.100
at the state of the world,
and will make the decision

00:06:13.100 --> 00:06:14.450
to schedule a new pot.

00:06:14.450 --> 00:06:16.190
You know, it expects
three, it sees two,

00:06:16.190 --> 00:06:17.360
so it'll create a second.

00:06:20.510 --> 00:06:22.420
So in that case, we get
our application back,

00:06:22.420 --> 00:06:23.320
everything is happy.

00:06:23.320 --> 00:06:26.170
But you know, this
is the lucky case.

00:06:26.170 --> 00:06:28.750
We were fortunate that
the zone that went down,

00:06:28.750 --> 00:06:30.370
the zone that we
had an issue with,

00:06:30.370 --> 00:06:33.854
happened to not be the
zone our master node is in.

00:06:33.854 --> 00:06:35.770
So what happens if that
master node goes down?

00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:42.610
So in this case, we still
have those running workloads.

00:06:42.610 --> 00:06:44.650
They still are remaining
on those healthy nodes,

00:06:44.650 --> 00:06:47.050
and Kubernetes is
designed to sit in this

00:06:47.050 --> 00:06:48.550
as sort of a steady state.

00:06:48.550 --> 00:06:50.200
The nodes won't
affect the workloads.

00:06:50.200 --> 00:06:52.576
They're only waiting
to be told what to do,

00:06:52.576 --> 00:06:54.200
but they don't have
anything new to do,

00:06:54.200 --> 00:06:56.830
so they'll sort of
remain in that mode.

00:06:56.830 --> 00:06:59.527
Traffic can continue to
enter the [? D ?] cluster,

00:06:59.527 --> 00:07:01.360
so if you're using the
global load balancer,

00:07:01.360 --> 00:07:03.430
it's doing health checks
on those down nodes,

00:07:03.430 --> 00:07:06.390
and won't route traffic to them.

00:07:06.390 --> 00:07:09.090
However where you will
experience a problem

00:07:09.090 --> 00:07:11.117
is when you have
internal services.

00:07:11.117 --> 00:07:13.200
So if you have an internal
Kubernetes service that

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:17.580
is in that downed node over
there-- that downed zone,

00:07:17.580 --> 00:07:20.520
the internal services may still
try to route to that machine.

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:22.920
And that's because the control
plane and the master node

00:07:22.920 --> 00:07:25.350
are responsible for
registering the endpoints that

00:07:25.350 --> 00:07:27.000
tell the services where to go.

00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:28.620
When I want to
connect to Redis, it

00:07:28.620 --> 00:07:31.710
tells me, OK, this is a hop that
you can go to that's available.

00:07:31.710 --> 00:07:33.470
And without the
control plane up, I'm

00:07:33.470 --> 00:07:35.400
going to have a
stale set of routes.

00:07:38.860 --> 00:07:42.010
So we see how a single
node can be problematic,

00:07:42.010 --> 00:07:45.030
and can result in this state.

00:07:45.030 --> 00:07:46.780
But Tina is going to
talk to you about how

00:07:46.780 --> 00:07:50.170
we want to solve some of that.

00:07:50.170 --> 00:07:52.830
TINA ZHANG: OK, so
in order to eliminate

00:07:52.830 --> 00:07:57.060
the single point of failure
that Jeff just described,

00:07:57.060 --> 00:07:59.610
you can use a GKE
regional cluster,

00:07:59.610 --> 00:08:04.950
which is a product that became
generally available in June.

00:08:04.950 --> 00:08:09.180
And with a regional cluster
you get a multi-master setup

00:08:09.180 --> 00:08:12.540
where there are three masters
in three different zones

00:08:12.540 --> 00:08:15.400
within a GCP region.

00:08:15.400 --> 00:08:17.670
So we have a load
balancer that's

00:08:17.670 --> 00:08:19.240
sitting in front
of these masters,

00:08:19.240 --> 00:08:21.720
and that serves as
the endpoint that's

00:08:21.720 --> 00:08:25.680
used to communicate
with the control plane.

00:08:25.680 --> 00:08:29.910
And by having three masters
behind your GKE cluster,

00:08:29.910 --> 00:08:34.679
we help to eliminate downtime
when one master becomes

00:08:34.679 --> 00:08:38.789
unavailable, either because it's
become into an unhealthy state,

00:08:38.789 --> 00:08:41.549
or because it's being upgraded.

00:08:41.549 --> 00:08:44.340
So when we perform
upgrades of the master,

00:08:44.340 --> 00:08:47.550
we upgrade one at a time so
you don't get the downtime

00:08:47.550 --> 00:08:51.540
that you would experience with
a zonal cluster deployment.

00:08:51.540 --> 00:08:53.130
And then when it
comes to the nodes,

00:08:53.130 --> 00:08:56.730
you could still choose to have
all of your nodes singly homed

00:08:56.730 --> 00:08:57.940
within one region--

00:08:57.940 --> 00:09:00.090
sorry, within one
zone, or you can

00:09:00.090 --> 00:09:02.705
use the multi-zonal
deployment that's shone

00:09:02.705 --> 00:09:06.240
here, which works much
the same as before.

00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:11.130
But of course ultimately, if
you have a multi-master setup

00:09:11.130 --> 00:09:13.980
and a multi-zonal
deployment, that

00:09:13.980 --> 00:09:17.130
doesn't automatically
mean that your application

00:09:17.130 --> 00:09:19.635
is high-availability.

00:09:19.635 --> 00:09:21.510
JEFF JOHNSON: So that
begs the question, what

00:09:21.510 --> 00:09:23.485
does high availability mean?

00:09:23.485 --> 00:09:25.110
I think when we talk
high availability,

00:09:25.110 --> 00:09:27.360
a lot of times we jump
towards solutions.

00:09:27.360 --> 00:09:29.430
We talk about putting
load balancers,

00:09:29.430 --> 00:09:31.680
and things like that-- failover.

00:09:31.680 --> 00:09:33.240
But simply put,
high availability

00:09:33.240 --> 00:09:35.940
means meeting some high SLO.

00:09:35.940 --> 00:09:38.430
In practice, that's
some number of nines

00:09:38.430 --> 00:09:39.900
that meets your business needs.

00:09:39.900 --> 00:09:44.620
And really there's many
ways to achieve HA.

00:09:44.620 --> 00:09:49.160
You could have, for instance,
a highly-available network

00:09:49.160 --> 00:09:51.610
connection that
you buy from an ISP

00:09:51.610 --> 00:09:53.530
because they gave you a
dedicated circuit that

00:09:53.530 --> 00:09:56.490
has an extremely high SLO.

00:09:56.490 --> 00:10:00.420
Or you could buy internet
from a couple of providers

00:10:00.420 --> 00:10:04.490
and perform failover
between them.

00:10:04.490 --> 00:10:08.000
When you're designing your
application to meet a high SLO,

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:10.700
it's important that you
understand all the pieces that

00:10:10.700 --> 00:10:12.920
make up that application,
and can understand where

00:10:12.920 --> 00:10:16.144
all the failures can happen.

00:10:16.144 --> 00:10:18.810
One way to split that up-- and I
think this works well for a lot

00:10:18.810 --> 00:10:21.687
of applications, but we'll say
in the context of Kubernetes

00:10:21.687 --> 00:10:23.520
--is to break it up
into these four layers--

00:10:23.520 --> 00:10:26.820
the control plane, the
networking, the application,

00:10:26.820 --> 00:10:30.570
and the persistence
underneath all that.

00:10:30.570 --> 00:10:33.900
We saw how a single
zone cluster on GKE

00:10:33.900 --> 00:10:37.150
could go down with a zone, and
the failures that can happen.

00:10:37.150 --> 00:10:40.120
So it's clearly not a
high-availability solution.

00:10:40.120 --> 00:10:42.630
But as cluster
administrators it's

00:10:42.630 --> 00:10:46.260
our responsibility to
ensure that our cluster--

00:10:46.260 --> 00:10:47.989
our control plane
--is running in a way

00:10:47.989 --> 00:10:49.155
that supports that failover.

00:10:51.917 --> 00:10:54.500
Tina is going to dive into some
of those pieces of the control

00:10:54.500 --> 00:10:55.970
plane-- into the
Kubernetes internals

00:10:55.970 --> 00:10:57.428
--to talk about
how we manage that.

00:10:59.780 --> 00:11:02.660
TINA ZHANG: OK, so taking
a closer look at what's

00:11:02.660 --> 00:11:05.900
happening with the
components on the masters

00:11:05.900 --> 00:11:09.030
when you're running a
GKE regional cluster.

00:11:09.030 --> 00:11:11.420
So here we're going to
show you how they interact

00:11:11.420 --> 00:11:13.340
with each other in
order to deliver

00:11:13.340 --> 00:11:16.942
this high-availability
control plane.

00:11:16.942 --> 00:11:20.990
So to start off, when it
comes to the API server,

00:11:20.990 --> 00:11:23.920
these all work in active mode.

00:11:23.920 --> 00:11:26.930
So all the API
servers are actively

00:11:26.930 --> 00:11:29.630
doing the work that's being
assigned to them by the load

00:11:29.630 --> 00:11:33.470
balancer, and this is possible
because the API server

00:11:33.470 --> 00:11:35.010
is a stateless component.

00:11:35.010 --> 00:11:38.150
So it doesn't matter which
individual API server

00:11:38.150 --> 00:11:39.800
is receiving the request.

00:11:39.800 --> 00:11:44.930
But they only talk to
their local etcd's.

00:11:44.930 --> 00:11:46.770
But when it comes
to etcd, things

00:11:46.770 --> 00:11:49.090
are a little bit more complex.

00:11:49.090 --> 00:11:51.540
So a group of etcd
instances together

00:11:51.540 --> 00:11:54.220
is called an etcd cluster.

00:11:54.220 --> 00:11:57.540
And because etcd is a
distributed key value

00:11:57.540 --> 00:11:59.700
store that's backed
by a database,

00:11:59.700 --> 00:12:01.980
there needs to be
leader election

00:12:01.980 --> 00:12:05.280
with a strict majority in order
for writes to the database

00:12:05.280 --> 00:12:06.750
to succeed.

00:12:06.750 --> 00:12:10.900
You might ask why here there
are actually two etcd clusters.

00:12:10.900 --> 00:12:14.550
This is because there
is a core etcd that

00:12:14.550 --> 00:12:18.600
has the main
Kubernetes objects that

00:12:18.600 --> 00:12:22.140
influence the functionality and
the operations of the cluster--

00:12:22.140 --> 00:12:24.030
that's etcd-0.

00:12:24.030 --> 00:12:26.400
And then there's a
separate etcd-1 cluster

00:12:26.400 --> 00:12:29.920
that's used just for
storing Kubernetes events.

00:12:29.920 --> 00:12:33.330
So these events objects
are providing an insight

00:12:33.330 --> 00:12:36.180
into what's happening inside
the cluster, such as a pod

00:12:36.180 --> 00:12:38.530
re-scheduling, or
a pod eviction,

00:12:38.530 --> 00:12:42.020
but they're not fundamental to
the functioning of the cluster,

00:12:42.020 --> 00:12:43.770
and you can imagine
that they are probably

00:12:43.770 --> 00:12:46.830
be quite high in
number, so we keep them

00:12:46.830 --> 00:12:51.210
in a separate etcd
cluster for that reason.

00:12:51.210 --> 00:12:53.700
And I want to spend a
little bit of time delving

00:12:53.700 --> 00:12:58.440
into why we have three
masters for regional clusters,

00:12:58.440 --> 00:13:01.110
and not two or four.

00:13:01.110 --> 00:13:05.130
And this is all related to how
etcd reaches Quorum in order

00:13:05.130 --> 00:13:07.870
to write to the database.

00:13:07.870 --> 00:13:11.920
So for writes to succeed
you need a strict majority,

00:13:11.920 --> 00:13:18.000
so, i.e., two out of three
must reach agreement.

00:13:18.000 --> 00:13:19.680
And with three
masters that means

00:13:19.680 --> 00:13:22.380
you can tolerate the
failure of one master,

00:13:22.380 --> 00:13:26.010
because the remaining two
healthy instances are still

00:13:26.010 --> 00:13:29.520
able to reach Quorum, so it
guarantees that there'll still

00:13:29.520 --> 00:13:33.400
be a majority of the cluster
that can continue to operate.

00:13:33.400 --> 00:13:36.270
However, if we only
had two masters,

00:13:36.270 --> 00:13:38.070
you would still
need both members

00:13:38.070 --> 00:13:41.280
to be healthy to reach
this strict majority,

00:13:41.280 --> 00:13:44.770
so you wouldn't have any
failover in that case.

00:13:44.770 --> 00:13:46.890
So it's worth
noting that actually

00:13:46.890 --> 00:13:51.450
during the period in which
a master is being upgraded

00:13:51.450 --> 00:13:56.850
or one master is unavailable,
your cluster control

00:13:56.850 --> 00:14:00.240
plane remains available,
but technically speaking,

00:14:00.240 --> 00:14:02.840
at that point in
time it's no longer

00:14:02.840 --> 00:14:06.780
a high-availability control
plane, because if there was

00:14:06.780 --> 00:14:10.500
a meteor strike on one of
the two remaining masters,

00:14:10.500 --> 00:14:13.020
writes to the database
would no longer succeed.

00:14:13.020 --> 00:14:16.980
So you can see from this example
that having an odd cluster size

00:14:16.980 --> 00:14:20.490
doesn't change the number
needed to reach a majority,

00:14:20.490 --> 00:14:23.790
but you gain extra
failure tolerance

00:14:23.790 --> 00:14:25.560
by adding the extra member.

00:14:25.560 --> 00:14:28.020
So you always want to
run an etch cluster

00:14:28.020 --> 00:14:30.610
with an odd number of members.

00:14:30.610 --> 00:14:33.700
So by running an etcd
cluster of size 3,

00:14:33.700 --> 00:14:35.220
this means that
our control plane

00:14:35.220 --> 00:14:37.140
is tolerant to the
failure of one machine,

00:14:37.140 --> 00:14:39.480
and of course it makes
sense to spread these

00:14:39.480 --> 00:14:42.730
across different locations.

00:14:42.730 --> 00:14:45.990
So for the other control plane
components-- the controller

00:14:45.990 --> 00:14:49.590
manager, the scheduler, as well
as the cluster auto-scaler,

00:14:49.590 --> 00:14:51.840
these all have
their own elections,

00:14:51.840 --> 00:14:54.760
but they operate in
active/passive mode.

00:14:54.760 --> 00:14:57.150
So only the leader
is doing any work,

00:14:57.150 --> 00:14:59.760
and it talks directly
to its local API

00:14:59.760 --> 00:15:01.830
server on the same master.

00:15:01.830 --> 00:15:04.410
There's also an add-on
manager leader election,

00:15:04.410 --> 00:15:07.470
and this just follows the
elected controller manager

00:15:07.470 --> 00:15:09.250
leader.

00:15:09.250 --> 00:15:11.610
JEFF JOHNSON: So that's one
piece of the overall pie,

00:15:11.610 --> 00:15:13.890
right, having a
highly-reliable control panel--

00:15:13.890 --> 00:15:16.010
or, sorry, control plane--

00:15:16.010 --> 00:15:17.460
panel's nice, too.

00:15:17.460 --> 00:15:21.120
But the network is also
a key piece of that.

00:15:21.120 --> 00:15:23.220
When we talk about high
availability and failover,

00:15:23.220 --> 00:15:24.994
that often meets load balancing.

00:15:24.994 --> 00:15:26.910
But when you're thinking
about load balancing,

00:15:26.910 --> 00:15:29.310
it's important that piece
is it's own single point

00:15:29.310 --> 00:15:31.200
of failure.

00:15:31.200 --> 00:15:34.590
Load balancing on GCP is not
like a traditional HA proxy

00:15:34.590 --> 00:15:37.950
machine, you know, backed maybe
by DNS Round Robin or something

00:15:37.950 --> 00:15:38.910
like that.

00:15:38.910 --> 00:15:41.460
GKE uses Network Load
Balancing, a product

00:15:41.460 --> 00:15:44.040
that's available for
you to use on GCE.

00:15:44.040 --> 00:15:47.460
That load balancing is backed
by a technology called Maglev.

00:15:47.460 --> 00:15:49.980
Maglev, there's a lot of
excellent papers out there

00:15:49.980 --> 00:15:52.940
that go into great
detail, but essentially it

00:15:52.940 --> 00:15:55.470
is a non-proxying
load balancer that's

00:15:55.470 --> 00:15:57.630
part of our
software-defined network,

00:15:57.630 --> 00:15:59.730
and it's the same
technology backing

00:15:59.730 --> 00:16:04.040
load balancing across lots
of products in Google.

00:16:04.040 --> 00:16:06.250
So what this diagram
represents are

00:16:06.250 --> 00:16:09.340
some of the fiber connections
between GCP's data centers.

00:16:09.340 --> 00:16:13.810
So Google lays a lot of its
own cable across the sea,

00:16:13.810 --> 00:16:17.650
and we run our network at
a pretty low utilization.

00:16:17.650 --> 00:16:20.260
What that gives us is the
ability to do failover.

00:16:20.260 --> 00:16:22.990
So let's say Godzilla pops
out somewhere in the middle

00:16:22.990 --> 00:16:25.150
there and takes one
or two cables down.

00:16:25.150 --> 00:16:29.660
We have headroom in the other
connections to send the data.

00:16:29.660 --> 00:16:33.400
So we're ready to do
those sorts of failover.

00:16:33.400 --> 00:16:35.080
That sort of
redundancy and headroom

00:16:35.080 --> 00:16:37.524
is critical to have
network reliability.

00:16:43.734 --> 00:16:45.400
TINA ZHANG: So we've
talked a little bit

00:16:45.400 --> 00:16:47.810
about how the control
plane operates

00:16:47.810 --> 00:16:50.260
in a high-availability scenario.

00:16:50.260 --> 00:16:52.780
Let's delve into
a bit of what GKE

00:16:52.780 --> 00:16:55.615
is doing for you under
the hood when we create

00:16:55.615 --> 00:16:59.210
a regional cluster,
and by extension,

00:16:59.210 --> 00:17:01.150
how you might go
about bootstrapping

00:17:01.150 --> 00:17:04.500
a high-availability cluster
yourself, because after all,

00:17:04.500 --> 00:17:09.250
Kubernetes is an open source
project, and many of our users

00:17:09.250 --> 00:17:11.950
actually-- well, many
of our Kubernetes users

00:17:11.950 --> 00:17:16.180
might choose to run their
own high-availability cluster

00:17:16.180 --> 00:17:20.829
directly on Google Compute
Engine, or on-prem in a data

00:17:20.829 --> 00:17:22.089
center.

00:17:22.089 --> 00:17:24.520
So "Kubernetes the
Hard Way" by Kelsey

00:17:24.520 --> 00:17:27.730
Hightower does outline
the steps of what

00:17:27.730 --> 00:17:30.700
you would need to do to roll
your own high-availability

00:17:30.700 --> 00:17:31.340
solution.

00:17:31.340 --> 00:17:34.390
We're not going to go
into all of that today.

00:17:34.390 --> 00:17:39.160
But there's also the Cluster
Lifecycle Special Interest

00:17:39.160 --> 00:17:43.450
Group in the open source project
that have developed a cube ADM

00:17:43.450 --> 00:17:46.330
tool that helps a lot with the
bootstrapping of the control

00:17:46.330 --> 00:17:47.280
plane.

00:17:47.280 --> 00:17:50.260
However, even with those
fantastic resources,

00:17:50.260 --> 00:17:53.170
you still have to do a lot
of the higher-level setup

00:17:53.170 --> 00:17:55.720
and configuration.

00:17:55.720 --> 00:17:58.180
Importantly you'd
still need to bootstrap

00:17:58.180 --> 00:18:01.900
your own high
availability etcd cluster.

00:18:01.900 --> 00:18:05.980
And the steps are again pretty
well-documented by CoreOS,

00:18:05.980 --> 00:18:08.560
who founded the etcd
Project, but they're still

00:18:08.560 --> 00:18:11.650
fairly involved,
and they include

00:18:11.650 --> 00:18:15.130
setting up additional firewall
rules and SSL certificates

00:18:15.130 --> 00:18:18.700
between the etcd servers
for communication,

00:18:18.700 --> 00:18:22.330
as well as setting up some
sort of high-availability proxy

00:18:22.330 --> 00:18:23.770
in front.

00:18:23.770 --> 00:18:26.810
And vital system pods
such as Cube DNS,

00:18:26.810 --> 00:18:28.900
you'd also need to
set those up to use

00:18:28.900 --> 00:18:33.730
auto-scaling and anti-affinity
rules to be distributed

00:18:33.730 --> 00:18:35.560
across multiple
nodes, and Jeff is

00:18:35.560 --> 00:18:38.330
going to describe that
a little bit later on.

00:18:38.330 --> 00:18:41.320
But once you have
everything initialized,

00:18:41.320 --> 00:18:46.150
managing etcd clusters can be a
significant complexity overhead

00:18:46.150 --> 00:18:47.360
in itself.

00:18:47.360 --> 00:18:50.320
And I think we in the
SRE team, and many others

00:18:50.320 --> 00:18:52.180
in the Kubernetes
open source community

00:18:52.180 --> 00:18:55.070
have found this
out the hard way.

00:18:55.070 --> 00:18:59.920
So historically we've run
zonal GKE clusters-- so just

00:18:59.920 --> 00:19:01.780
with one etcd replica.

00:19:01.780 --> 00:19:04.270
We've had issues
due to etcd crash

00:19:04.270 --> 00:19:10.180
looping after a restart due to a
bug in right ahead log reading.

00:19:10.180 --> 00:19:12.610
There's also a bug in
the version of etcd

00:19:12.610 --> 00:19:16.630
we were using where the database
size would become increasingly

00:19:16.630 --> 00:19:19.780
bloated and de-fragmented.

00:19:19.780 --> 00:19:24.020
And with regional clusters
we have the added complexity

00:19:24.020 --> 00:19:27.370
of leader election.

00:19:27.370 --> 00:19:30.400
So we had some
regional-specific etcd bugs

00:19:30.400 --> 00:19:35.170
where the etcd-1 events were
not garbage collected properly,

00:19:35.170 --> 00:19:40.660
and where you could have an
etcd member lagging behind

00:19:40.660 --> 00:19:44.080
in database snapshots, so
not being fully caught up

00:19:44.080 --> 00:19:46.820
with the rest of the cluster.

00:19:46.820 --> 00:19:50.260
So what we also found was
that the steps involved

00:19:50.260 --> 00:19:55.630
in fixing an etcd replica that's
part of a three-member cluster

00:19:55.630 --> 00:20:01.002
is also a lot more involved than
just fixing one etcd replica.

00:20:01.002 --> 00:20:04.420
etcd is also extremely
sensitive to disk I/O,

00:20:04.420 --> 00:20:07.930
so in the open source
version of this talk

00:20:07.930 --> 00:20:10.300
we always recommend
to not provision

00:20:10.300 --> 00:20:13.660
the resources too
conservatively because when

00:20:13.660 --> 00:20:18.580
etcd becomes overloaded you
really need that disk capacity.

00:20:18.580 --> 00:20:20.290
But don't just take
our word for it.

00:20:20.290 --> 00:20:23.140
As I mentioned, there's
a lot of KubeCon talks

00:20:23.140 --> 00:20:26.500
on this topic from us
and also other people

00:20:26.500 --> 00:20:28.360
managing their own clusters.

00:20:28.360 --> 00:20:32.200
But with GKE the good news
is that the cluster creation

00:20:32.200 --> 00:20:35.780
process happens behind the
scenes at a click of a button.

00:20:35.780 --> 00:20:38.430
And once your cluster
is up and running,

00:20:38.430 --> 00:20:41.440
your friendly SRE team are
responsible for managing

00:20:41.440 --> 00:20:43.610
the control plane
components on your behalf.

00:20:52.259 --> 00:20:54.800
JEFF JOHNSON: So now that you
have a little bit of background

00:20:54.800 --> 00:20:57.260
on the top two layers
of the stack, what

00:20:57.260 --> 00:20:59.846
you get from using GKE, let's
talk about the next two.

00:20:59.846 --> 00:21:01.220
Let's talk about
your application

00:21:01.220 --> 00:21:03.040
and the persistence.

00:21:03.040 --> 00:21:06.600
A reliable Kubernetes
cluster is a table state

00:21:06.600 --> 00:21:10.520
for high availability, but
it's not a silver bullet.

00:21:10.520 --> 00:21:12.050
As an application
developer, it's

00:21:12.050 --> 00:21:14.420
your responsibility to ensure
that your application is

00:21:14.420 --> 00:21:18.750
deployed in a way that's
ready to handle failure.

00:21:18.750 --> 00:21:21.900
Your persistence
should be resilient

00:21:21.900 --> 00:21:22.920
to single-zone failure.

00:21:22.920 --> 00:21:27.690
So on GCE-- or GKE or GCE, if
you're using persistent disks,

00:21:27.690 --> 00:21:29.160
that means using
a regional disk,

00:21:29.160 --> 00:21:31.710
and picking two zones to
do replication across.

00:21:31.710 --> 00:21:33.685
I'll show how to
do that in a bit.

00:21:33.685 --> 00:21:35.310
And if you're using
other data services

00:21:35.310 --> 00:21:38.400
within GCP such as Cloud
SQL, you to make sure

00:21:38.400 --> 00:21:41.040
that they're configured in a
highly available configuration

00:21:41.040 --> 00:21:45.760
and support failover
your application

00:21:45.760 --> 00:21:48.235
should be running in a mode
that is ready for failure.

00:21:48.990 --> 00:21:52.300
So for instance, just like
the Kubernetes API server,

00:21:52.300 --> 00:21:54.710
if you're running in
an active active mode,

00:21:54.710 --> 00:21:56.650
That means that any
one of your instances

00:21:56.650 --> 00:21:59.020
are ready to handle
traffic at any time.

00:21:59.020 --> 00:22:01.960
This is a excellent pattern
for stateless applications.

00:22:01.960 --> 00:22:04.930
And it's generally very easy to
just turn the replica knob up.

00:22:04.930 --> 00:22:08.560
But it doesn't work for a lot of
other applications, especially

00:22:08.560 --> 00:22:09.740
stateful applications.

00:22:09.740 --> 00:22:12.490
So there are other patterns,
such as active passive,

00:22:12.490 --> 00:22:13.630
you can utilize.

00:22:13.630 --> 00:22:15.790
In an active passive mode--

00:22:15.790 --> 00:22:17.397
much like our
controller manager--

00:22:17.397 --> 00:22:19.480
you've got one instance
that is doing all the work

00:22:19.480 --> 00:22:21.130
and a few that are
sort of sitting idly

00:22:21.130 --> 00:22:24.520
by ready to be summoned.

00:22:24.520 --> 00:22:27.310
That requires a lot
more orchestration.

00:22:27.310 --> 00:22:29.680
And I'll show you how
you can sort of achieve

00:22:29.680 --> 00:22:32.920
a little bit of that with
a little bit of downtime

00:22:32.920 --> 00:22:35.220
by using Kubernetes.

00:22:35.220 --> 00:22:37.740
So with all of that
background and a week

00:22:37.740 --> 00:22:40.450
of hearing that word Kubernetes,
let's jump into a demo

00:22:40.450 --> 00:22:43.660
where we actually
make some stuff fail.

00:22:43.660 --> 00:22:45.750
So if you've ever seen
a Kubernetes demo,

00:22:45.750 --> 00:22:48.270
no doubt you've
seen the Guestbook.

00:22:48.270 --> 00:22:50.520
The Guestbook is a
bit of a toy app.

00:22:50.520 --> 00:22:54.330
But it actually represents a lot
of common patterns seen in just

00:22:54.330 --> 00:22:55.600
about every application.

00:22:55.600 --> 00:22:57.270
So if you haven't
seen the Guestbook,

00:22:57.270 --> 00:22:59.340
the Guestbook is like a ledger.

00:22:59.340 --> 00:23:01.310
You can write a message.

00:23:01.310 --> 00:23:04.850
Well, we've got
some saved there.

00:23:04.850 --> 00:23:07.580
And it is persisted
through refreshes.

00:23:07.580 --> 00:23:09.128
Good, it's working.

00:23:13.400 --> 00:23:15.092
But to really understand
what's going on

00:23:15.092 --> 00:23:16.550
and apply this to
your application,

00:23:16.550 --> 00:23:19.610
we need to look at the services
that make up the application.

00:23:19.610 --> 00:23:22.670
First, I will, of course,
reconnect to my GCEVM

00:23:22.670 --> 00:23:23.470
so we can show you.

00:23:26.840 --> 00:23:28.950
If you're interested in
looking at the Guestbook,

00:23:28.950 --> 00:23:31.130
the Guestbook is in
the Kubernetes repo,

00:23:31.130 --> 00:23:36.300
the main Kubernetes
repo under Examples.

00:23:36.300 --> 00:23:39.660
And what I did was I took the
YAML that's deploying it today,

00:23:39.660 --> 00:23:42.450
and I made a few tweaks
to support failover.

00:23:42.450 --> 00:23:44.580
So let's look at how
it's deployed first.

00:23:53.680 --> 00:23:55.540
So this is a bit of
an eye chart here.

00:23:55.540 --> 00:23:56.950
So I apologize for that.

00:23:56.950 --> 00:23:59.470
But I'll sort of walk you
through the groups here.

00:23:59.470 --> 00:24:02.400
The top three nodes, the top
three pods are the front end.

00:24:02.400 --> 00:24:05.230
That's a serverless web
application and a Docker

00:24:05.230 --> 00:24:05.980
container.

00:24:05.980 --> 00:24:08.380
It happens to be
a PHP application.

00:24:08.380 --> 00:24:10.210
I'm throwing no shade on that.

00:24:10.210 --> 00:24:14.500
That was my first
language besides English.

00:24:14.500 --> 00:24:17.410
And if you look,
that far right column

00:24:17.410 --> 00:24:22.370
in the Node section, those
are across two separate nodes.

00:24:22.370 --> 00:24:25.310
The three instances are
across two separate nodes.

00:24:25.310 --> 00:24:28.205
Those two separate nodes are
deployed to different zones.

00:24:39.440 --> 00:24:42.160
So we have one of those
nodes in central 1c

00:24:42.160 --> 00:24:43.180
and one in central 1f.

00:24:45.810 --> 00:24:48.410
The next set of pods here
is the redis-follower.

00:24:48.410 --> 00:24:52.450
The redis-follower is a
read-only copy of the database.

00:24:52.450 --> 00:24:55.330
So the persistence
doesn't really matter.

00:24:55.330 --> 00:24:57.660
It will get in sync with
the leader when we ask it,

00:24:57.660 --> 00:24:58.600
when we query for it.

00:24:58.600 --> 00:25:00.766
So whenever we do renders
of the Guestbook, whenever

00:25:00.766 --> 00:25:04.380
we do views, we're going
to ask those databases what

00:25:04.380 --> 00:25:05.790
the state of the world is.

00:25:05.790 --> 00:25:07.320
But when we need
to do rights, we

00:25:07.320 --> 00:25:09.072
want to talk to redis-leader.

00:25:09.072 --> 00:25:12.120
Redis-leader is the
center of truth.

00:25:12.120 --> 00:25:13.680
And that means that
its persistence

00:25:13.680 --> 00:25:15.210
is really important.

00:25:15.210 --> 00:25:18.390
If a zone went down and we
couldn't get the disk back

00:25:18.390 --> 00:25:20.070
from that zone, well,
then essentially

00:25:20.070 --> 00:25:24.180
our application is in a
state where it's not useful.

00:25:24.180 --> 00:25:28.350
But a bonus that we get by
having those read-only replicas

00:25:28.350 --> 00:25:31.110
running on separate nodes as
well is that if one of those

00:25:31.110 --> 00:25:34.380
goes down, in the mean
time while the leader is

00:25:34.380 --> 00:25:37.230
being rescheduled, we still have
a full copy of our database.

00:25:37.230 --> 00:25:38.700
We can still do reads.

00:25:38.700 --> 00:25:42.540
We may just have to wait a
little bit to do commits.

00:25:42.540 --> 00:25:44.234
So let's take this thing down.

00:25:44.234 --> 00:25:45.900
I want to make sure
we take down the one

00:25:45.900 --> 00:25:47.895
with the redis-follower.

00:25:47.895 --> 00:25:49.020
So let's do a little watch.

00:26:01.740 --> 00:26:04.730
That follows on DG00, of course.

00:26:04.730 --> 00:26:05.640
Sorry, leader.

00:26:05.640 --> 00:26:06.990
And that is central 1c.

00:26:06.990 --> 00:26:07.490
OK.

00:26:14.180 --> 00:26:16.850
So fortunately for you,
I do not have the ability

00:26:16.850 --> 00:26:19.167
to actually shut
down a GCE zone.

00:26:19.167 --> 00:26:21.500
I could see where that would
be a concern as a customer.

00:26:21.500 --> 00:26:23.930
But what I do have
is a Kubernetes API.

00:26:23.930 --> 00:26:25.490
And what the API
lets me do is it

00:26:25.490 --> 00:26:27.290
lets me mark a
node as unscheduled

00:26:27.290 --> 00:26:30.740
and then immediately evict
all the workloads off of that.

00:26:30.740 --> 00:26:33.560
If you're playing along at home,
you can run these same commands

00:26:33.560 --> 00:26:34.970
there.

00:26:34.970 --> 00:26:38.030
Drain is the one you want to
start evicting the workloads.

00:26:38.030 --> 00:26:42.325
Or you could do a GCloud
compute instance delete.

00:26:42.325 --> 00:26:44.450
The reason why I'm just
evicting them right here is

00:26:44.450 --> 00:26:46.850
that it does take a
little bit of time

00:26:46.850 --> 00:26:49.195
for that node to come down
and Kubernetes needs to go,

00:26:49.195 --> 00:26:51.070
all right, it's time to
reschedule that work.

00:26:51.070 --> 00:26:53.750
And that's because it's
also designed for failure.

00:26:53.750 --> 00:26:55.880
It's going to have some
loops before it takes.

00:26:55.880 --> 00:26:58.400
But we're in a demo, we
want to see action fast.

00:26:58.400 --> 00:27:00.740
And while I blabbed
over that whole thing,

00:27:00.740 --> 00:27:03.320
we can see the redis-leader
was rescheduled

00:27:03.320 --> 00:27:06.950
it's been running now for 38
seconds some of our other pods

00:27:06.950 --> 00:27:08.155
have been rescheduled.

00:27:08.155 --> 00:27:10.280
And the ones that were
running on that healthy node

00:27:10.280 --> 00:27:12.940
that we didn't mess with have
been running for three hours

00:27:12.940 --> 00:27:14.390
when I woke up this morning.

00:27:17.337 --> 00:27:18.920
And let's hope this
thing still works.

00:27:24.700 --> 00:27:26.810
OK that's a good sign.

00:27:26.810 --> 00:27:28.149
So this looks like a toy.

00:27:28.149 --> 00:27:30.190
This is simple, but what
we've actually done here

00:27:30.190 --> 00:27:32.610
is we've taken down
a machine in a zone.

00:27:32.610 --> 00:27:35.440
And let's focus on
the redis-leader,

00:27:35.440 --> 00:27:36.670
We took it down on a zone.

00:27:36.670 --> 00:27:37.990
It had an attached disk.

00:27:37.990 --> 00:27:40.630
That disk contained the
truth of our application.

00:27:40.630 --> 00:27:43.720
We moved it to a new
machine in a different zone

00:27:43.720 --> 00:27:44.590
that was happy.

00:27:44.590 --> 00:27:45.850
We reattached that disk.

00:27:45.850 --> 00:27:49.469
And it came up in a
short period of time.

00:27:49.469 --> 00:27:51.010
Throughout that
time, our application

00:27:51.010 --> 00:27:52.450
would have been accessible.

00:27:52.450 --> 00:27:54.640
We would have been able to
ingress into our cluster.

00:27:54.640 --> 00:27:56.560
While that red as leader
was being rescheduled,

00:27:56.560 --> 00:27:58.309
we wouldn't have been
able to make rights.

00:27:58.309 --> 00:28:00.562
But we can still keep
our page up and healthy.

00:28:00.562 --> 00:28:02.020
And that's a tradeoff
that you need

00:28:02.020 --> 00:28:06.190
to make when you're considering
your application deployment.

00:28:06.190 --> 00:28:07.900
This, by the way,
isn't what you'll get

00:28:07.900 --> 00:28:09.160
if you deploy the Guestbook.

00:28:09.160 --> 00:28:12.000
If you go to Kubernetes
examples Guestbook,

00:28:12.000 --> 00:28:13.750
that's not what you'll
get out of the box.

00:28:13.750 --> 00:28:14.950
Let's look at some
of the changes

00:28:14.950 --> 00:28:16.030
I made to make that work.

00:28:24.720 --> 00:28:26.570
So let's look at the front end.

00:28:28.925 --> 00:28:30.300
So the front end,
as I mentioned,

00:28:30.300 --> 00:28:32.890
is a stateless application.

00:28:32.890 --> 00:28:34.760
This is the YAML
section of the talk

00:28:34.760 --> 00:28:37.100
so it might be
extra hard to see.

00:28:42.337 --> 00:28:44.170
Oh yeah, doesn't get a
lot bigger than that.

00:28:44.170 --> 00:28:47.175
So it is a Kubernetes
deployment with three replicas.

00:28:47.175 --> 00:28:49.300
And that's where you say,
I've got a bunch of pods.

00:28:49.300 --> 00:28:50.999
Run three copies of it.

00:28:50.999 --> 00:28:52.540
Out of the box,
Kubernetes it doesn't

00:28:52.540 --> 00:28:55.810
make any scheduling guarantees
about where those go so.

00:28:55.810 --> 00:28:57.940
It could just as well
stick all those replicas

00:28:57.940 --> 00:28:59.540
on the same node.

00:28:59.540 --> 00:29:03.760
If you're on a high replica
account to handle failover,

00:29:03.760 --> 00:29:05.240
that's really not
going to cut it.

00:29:05.240 --> 00:29:08.260
That's just one
piece of the puzzle.

00:29:08.260 --> 00:29:10.960
What you can do, though, is
you can hint at Kubernetes.

00:29:10.960 --> 00:29:12.830
You can tell it what you want.

00:29:12.830 --> 00:29:15.850
And if we look at
the bottom the way

00:29:15.850 --> 00:29:19.120
I've done that is using
pod anti-affinity.

00:29:19.120 --> 00:29:21.870
Pod anti-affinity
either tells or hints

00:29:21.870 --> 00:29:25.130
at the scheduler to schedule
your pods in some way.

00:29:25.130 --> 00:29:29.410
So if you dive down the
tree a little bit here,

00:29:29.410 --> 00:29:33.250
we've specified the same
metadata, the same app

00:29:33.250 --> 00:29:36.880
selector, app Guestbook
to your front end.

00:29:36.880 --> 00:29:40.510
And we specified this failure
domain key, that being this

00:29:40.510 --> 00:29:42.820
is zone key.

00:29:42.820 --> 00:29:44.290
So this is where
you are starting

00:29:44.290 --> 00:29:46.165
to think about what you
want your application

00:29:46.165 --> 00:29:47.530
to be resilient to.

00:29:47.530 --> 00:29:49.564
This could also be a machine.

00:29:49.564 --> 00:29:50.980
And this will tell
Kubernetes, OK,

00:29:50.980 --> 00:29:55.187
if a pod is already scheduled
on this zone or this machine,

00:29:55.187 --> 00:29:56.770
try not to schedule
another one there.

00:30:07.530 --> 00:30:10.370
The redis--follower is
deployed in the same way.

00:30:10.370 --> 00:30:13.030
I'm not retaining its
persistence across,

00:30:13.030 --> 00:30:16.740
because it is able to
catch itself up to speed.

00:30:16.740 --> 00:30:20.010
We could try doing that,
and it should work.

00:30:20.010 --> 00:30:24.480
But if you don't need to make
it that complex, don't, let

00:30:24.480 --> 00:30:27.780
your testing and let your
evaluation of the reliability

00:30:27.780 --> 00:30:31.287
inform those sort of decisions.

00:30:31.287 --> 00:30:32.870
One thing I want to
point out, though,

00:30:32.870 --> 00:30:35.540
is that we've used
prefer schedule.

00:30:35.540 --> 00:30:37.610
You can tell
communities absolutely

00:30:37.610 --> 00:30:43.680
do not schedule this node if
it meets these conditions.

00:30:43.680 --> 00:30:46.710
But what would happen in
that failure scenario is

00:30:46.710 --> 00:30:49.350
when we lost a zone,
sure, we'll move over

00:30:49.350 --> 00:30:52.980
the work that can be moved
over like singletons.

00:30:52.980 --> 00:30:54.810
But we're not going to
reschedule the nodes

00:30:54.810 --> 00:30:56.130
that you'd expect.

00:30:56.130 --> 00:30:57.780
We're not going to
say, OK, well let's

00:30:57.780 --> 00:31:00.870
shift all these leaders over
here to this healthy zone.

00:31:00.870 --> 00:31:03.600
So we won't actually be able
to serve the same capacity

00:31:03.600 --> 00:31:05.154
that we could before.

00:31:05.154 --> 00:31:06.570
It's important to
be aware of this

00:31:06.570 --> 00:31:08.174
as you're planning
your deployment.

00:31:11.080 --> 00:31:15.173
The most interesting one to
show off is the redis-leader.

00:31:19.430 --> 00:31:21.560
The redis-leader is
deployed as a StatefulSet.

00:31:21.560 --> 00:31:23.780
And I changed that from
being a deployment.

00:31:23.780 --> 00:31:26.030
And what StatefulSet
allows us to do here

00:31:26.030 --> 00:31:29.960
is specify a volume
claim template.

00:31:29.960 --> 00:31:33.500
Volume claim template
says, OK, I need a disk.

00:31:33.500 --> 00:31:36.350
And the disk has this sort of
storage class, just a name,

00:31:36.350 --> 00:31:38.990
just to string
that I picked here.

00:31:38.990 --> 00:31:42.260
And Kubernetes will
reliably attach this disk,

00:31:42.260 --> 00:31:44.450
or attempt to attach
this disk too my node

00:31:44.450 --> 00:31:47.570
and eventually to my pod.

00:31:47.570 --> 00:31:50.180
So the volume mounting
is redis-data.

00:31:50.180 --> 00:31:52.487
And it's in the slash data
directory of the container,

00:31:52.487 --> 00:31:53.070
nothing crazy.

00:31:55.804 --> 00:31:57.220
But the storage
class is something

00:31:57.220 --> 00:31:58.910
that you're creating
in your planning.

00:31:58.910 --> 00:32:01.330
So let's take a look at how
I define this storage class.

00:32:05.450 --> 00:32:09.420
For the storage class, you
specify a name, a provisioner--

00:32:09.420 --> 00:32:12.240
so I'm using a GCE
persistent disk--

00:32:12.240 --> 00:32:14.350
and then the parameters
for that provisioner.

00:32:14.350 --> 00:32:16.620
And the important
ones to look at today

00:32:16.620 --> 00:32:18.360
are the replication type.

00:32:18.360 --> 00:32:20.400
We're using a regional PD.

00:32:20.400 --> 00:32:23.040
And then we're specifying two
zones for that regional PD

00:32:23.040 --> 00:32:24.360
to replicate to.

00:32:24.360 --> 00:32:27.710
So regional PDs allow us
to say, across these zones,

00:32:27.710 --> 00:32:30.210
I want to make sure that my
data is always being replicated.

00:32:30.210 --> 00:32:31.793
So if I do it right
here, it is always

00:32:31.793 --> 00:32:34.670
available on this other zone.

00:32:34.670 --> 00:32:39.260
These zones don't necessarily
need to coincide with your VM.

00:32:39.260 --> 00:32:41.420
But for performance, it's
probably a good idea.

00:32:41.420 --> 00:32:43.656
And when you think about
your exposure to failure,

00:32:43.656 --> 00:32:45.905
it's probably a good idea
to be aware of that as well.

00:32:56.130 --> 00:32:56.870
All right.

00:32:56.870 --> 00:33:00.410
So now that we've seen a demo
in action, let's sort of review

00:33:00.410 --> 00:33:03.590
some of those core
concepts and best practices

00:33:03.590 --> 00:33:07.650
for your own application.

00:33:07.650 --> 00:33:08.655
Back on the slides.

00:33:11.240 --> 00:33:11.740
Thank you.

00:33:16.390 --> 00:33:19.210
So regional clusters are
a piece of the puzzle

00:33:19.210 --> 00:33:20.979
to handle zonal failures.

00:33:20.979 --> 00:33:23.020
But they give you a lot
of everyday improvements,

00:33:23.020 --> 00:33:25.670
like zero downtime
upgrades and resizes.

00:33:25.670 --> 00:33:27.170
So something you
should be aware of.

00:33:27.170 --> 00:33:31.270
If you resize your cluster
from one worker node to 100,

00:33:31.270 --> 00:33:35.582
we're going to resize the master
node backing in our project.

00:33:35.582 --> 00:33:37.040
Because there's a
lot more traffic,

00:33:37.040 --> 00:33:39.780
a 100 nodes actually, 100x.

00:33:39.780 --> 00:33:44.180
And in that scenario, because
we roll out changes one by one

00:33:44.180 --> 00:33:46.681
in a regional cluster, you
shouldn't see a lot of downtime

00:33:46.681 --> 00:33:47.180
in that.

00:33:47.180 --> 00:33:50.100
That should be a
graceful failover.

00:33:50.100 --> 00:33:53.030
As if GA, regional
clusters are free.

00:33:53.030 --> 00:33:55.430
So there isn't really
a concern about cost

00:33:55.430 --> 00:33:58.850
in terms of having a
bunch of master nodes.

00:33:58.850 --> 00:34:01.070
But node locations
are really something

00:34:01.070 --> 00:34:02.430
you should be aware of.

00:34:02.430 --> 00:34:05.630
And a reason to be
aware of that is around

00:34:05.630 --> 00:34:08.159
crosses zonal traffic.

00:34:08.159 --> 00:34:11.150
So if you have an application
that is particularly latency

00:34:11.150 --> 00:34:14.659
sensitive, that's doing a lot of
interest service communication,

00:34:14.659 --> 00:34:17.840
you might be used to
that one millisecond

00:34:17.840 --> 00:34:22.310
in the 95th percentile
timing, that sort of latency.

00:34:22.310 --> 00:34:24.634
But it's going to be increased
it's going across zone.

00:34:24.634 --> 00:34:26.300
Like Tina mentioned
earlier, think of it

00:34:26.300 --> 00:34:28.130
as going out of one
building in the campus

00:34:28.130 --> 00:34:30.850
and going to another.

00:34:30.850 --> 00:34:33.260
Another concern is
cost of that traffic.

00:34:33.260 --> 00:34:35.469
So when you're running
traffic within your zone,

00:34:35.469 --> 00:34:37.372
you're not being
billed for that.

00:34:37.372 --> 00:34:38.830
But when you cross
the zones, there

00:34:38.830 --> 00:34:40.280
is billing associated with that.

00:34:40.280 --> 00:34:42.196
So if you're doing
something like pulling down

00:34:42.196 --> 00:34:44.139
a lot of database
results, if you're

00:34:44.139 --> 00:34:46.690
doing something like
streaming a lot of log data,

00:34:46.690 --> 00:34:47.449
that can add up.

00:34:47.449 --> 00:34:49.449
And it's something you
should be aware of as you

00:34:49.449 --> 00:34:51.580
plan your deployment.

00:34:51.580 --> 00:34:54.166
Technologies like Istio,
like a service mash

00:34:54.166 --> 00:34:56.290
can help you to sort of
inform these and plan these

00:34:56.290 --> 00:34:57.290
out a little bit better.

00:35:01.370 --> 00:35:05.750
A few things just to be aware of
that are really more edge cases

00:35:05.750 --> 00:35:09.680
is that when you spread
across multiple zones,

00:35:09.680 --> 00:35:11.960
you're exposing yourself
more to zonal issues.

00:35:11.960 --> 00:35:13.670
The simple fact that
you're in more zones

00:35:13.670 --> 00:35:18.290
means that you might be in that
unlucky asteroid strike, right?

00:35:18.290 --> 00:35:20.060
Things like upgrades,
creates, and deletes

00:35:20.060 --> 00:35:22.440
could be impacted by that.

00:35:22.440 --> 00:35:25.230
It will certainly take more
time to roll those changes out,

00:35:25.230 --> 00:35:28.510
because we do those one by
one instead of in one shot.

00:35:28.510 --> 00:35:30.420
So if you have an
environment where

00:35:30.420 --> 00:35:33.457
you're creating GKE
clusters very rapidly,

00:35:33.457 --> 00:35:35.040
you should be aware
of that, something

00:35:35.040 --> 00:35:36.765
like a CI environment.

00:35:39.560 --> 00:35:41.930
If you're running a big
cluster, it's not a guarantee

00:35:41.930 --> 00:35:44.600
that you can spin up 500
nodes immediately or 1,000

00:35:44.600 --> 00:35:46.130
nodes immediately.

00:35:46.130 --> 00:35:49.230
So you may run into quota
limits, for example.

00:35:49.230 --> 00:35:51.860
So it's important to just like
your applications are reserving

00:35:51.860 --> 00:35:54.080
for capacity and
failure that you also

00:35:54.080 --> 00:35:55.520
have some node
capacity if that's

00:35:55.520 --> 00:35:57.670
what you're designing for.

00:35:57.670 --> 00:36:01.730
Now at the moment, you can't
migrate between a zonal cluster

00:36:01.730 --> 00:36:02.900
and a regional cluster.

00:36:02.900 --> 00:36:04.910
So it's a choice you
have to make upfront.

00:36:07.540 --> 00:36:09.020
So with that
background the team is

00:36:09.020 --> 00:36:10.900
going to talk to
you about how these

00:36:10.900 --> 00:36:13.010
clusters healthy and reliable.

00:36:13.010 --> 00:36:15.000
TINA ZHANG: OK thanks, Jeff.

00:36:15.000 --> 00:36:18.460
Yeah, so we've demonstrated
how the GKE regional clusters

00:36:18.460 --> 00:36:22.812
products can make your
GKE cluster mode reliable.

00:36:22.812 --> 00:36:25.090
So well, how are
Google engineers

00:36:25.090 --> 00:36:26.830
keeping their side
of the bargain

00:36:26.830 --> 00:36:30.400
to help make the
GKE system reliable?

00:36:30.400 --> 00:36:32.110
This is a challenge
for us, particularly

00:36:32.110 --> 00:36:36.320
as the adoption in
GKE continues to grow,

00:36:36.320 --> 00:36:39.810
as well as the number
of GCP locations.

00:36:39.810 --> 00:36:43.030
So in SRE the team, we're
responsible for managing

00:36:43.030 --> 00:36:46.270
the fleet of GKE hosted
masters and making sure

00:36:46.270 --> 00:36:48.910
that the Kubernetes control
plane components are

00:36:48.910 --> 00:36:50.980
up and running, as
well as managing

00:36:50.980 --> 00:36:55.750
the upgrade cycle of the masters
and ultimately protecting

00:36:55.750 --> 00:36:59.680
the service level objectives
to ensure a positive customer

00:36:59.680 --> 00:37:02.630
experience for our users.

00:37:02.630 --> 00:37:05.540
So we do this by holding
the pager for the product.

00:37:05.540 --> 00:37:09.970
But we also help to work with
feature launches, the release

00:37:09.970 --> 00:37:13.810
process, and general product
designs with our developer

00:37:13.810 --> 00:37:16.630
partners, as well as
other teams within Google

00:37:16.630 --> 00:37:19.310
that GKE is reliant on.

00:37:19.310 --> 00:37:21.430
And we have teams in
Seattle and London

00:37:21.430 --> 00:37:24.580
providing 24/7 on-call coverage.

00:37:24.580 --> 00:37:28.810
And ultimately, the aim of all
site reliability engineering

00:37:28.810 --> 00:37:31.840
teams is to automate
our own jobs away,

00:37:31.840 --> 00:37:35.470
much in the same vein
as the Kubernetes

00:37:35.470 --> 00:37:39.370
self-healing philosophy so that
the system can detect and heal

00:37:39.370 --> 00:37:41.920
itself without
manual intervention.

00:37:41.920 --> 00:37:45.340
And we do this by iterating
on pages and outages

00:37:45.340 --> 00:37:49.460
to make sure that they
don't happen repeatedly.

00:37:49.460 --> 00:37:51.590
So in order to do
this, we need to have

00:37:51.590 --> 00:37:55.070
good observability
and strong insights

00:37:55.070 --> 00:37:57.200
into the system behavior.

00:37:57.200 --> 00:38:02.180
So we care both about the
GKE platform as well as

00:38:02.180 --> 00:38:04.050
every single cluster.

00:38:04.050 --> 00:38:07.140
We do this by gathering metrics
on both of those aspects

00:38:07.140 --> 00:38:09.440
and visualizing
them on dashboards

00:38:09.440 --> 00:38:12.980
using an internal Google version
that's been around for years,

00:38:12.980 --> 00:38:15.010
used by all SRE teams.

00:38:15.010 --> 00:38:16.670
But it's pretty
similar to how you

00:38:16.670 --> 00:38:20.870
might use Promethus or
Grafana for metric gathering

00:38:20.870 --> 00:38:24.390
and visualization in
your own clusters.

00:38:24.390 --> 00:38:26.840
And we also keep an
aggregated view of this

00:38:26.840 --> 00:38:29.360
so that we can see
fleet-wide differences

00:38:29.360 --> 00:38:35.060
between cloud locations and
different Kubernetes versions.

00:38:35.060 --> 00:38:36.980
And finally, we
have some probers

00:38:36.980 --> 00:38:41.270
running outside of Google Cloud
Platform that are constantly

00:38:41.270 --> 00:38:43.680
creating and deleting clusters.

00:38:43.680 --> 00:38:46.670
So this helps to give us
a critical black box view

00:38:46.670 --> 00:38:50.230
into what a user would see.

00:38:50.230 --> 00:38:53.640
So in order to gather this
data in every cloud location

00:38:53.640 --> 00:38:58.590
we have a job running outside
of GCP called the monitoring

00:38:58.590 --> 00:38:59.370
server.

00:38:59.370 --> 00:39:03.390
And this collects metrics,
metadata, and core component

00:39:03.390 --> 00:39:05.610
logs from each master.

00:39:05.610 --> 00:39:08.580
So our definition of
master availability

00:39:08.580 --> 00:39:12.240
is based on Cube CTL
component states is returning

00:39:12.240 --> 00:39:14.830
healthy for every component.

00:39:14.830 --> 00:39:18.180
And if availability is down
for a short period of time,

00:39:18.180 --> 00:39:20.700
the monitoring server
will automatically

00:39:20.700 --> 00:39:26.220
recreate the unavailable
or unhealthy master VM.

00:39:26.220 --> 00:39:29.670
And it can also resize
the master VM in order

00:39:29.670 --> 00:39:31.950
to handle higher workloads.

00:39:31.950 --> 00:39:35.400
And if these actions don't
work and the master continues

00:39:35.400 --> 00:39:38.520
to remain unavailable,
an alert is sent

00:39:38.520 --> 00:39:41.130
to your friendly on-caller.

00:39:41.130 --> 00:39:43.500
This can also happen if
some other metrics get

00:39:43.500 --> 00:39:47.220
into a bad state according to
some predefined conditions,

00:39:47.220 --> 00:39:50.310
such as ET CD
backups being too old

00:39:50.310 --> 00:39:53.070
or master of VM
disks being too full.

00:39:53.070 --> 00:39:55.410
And depending on the
severity of the condition,

00:39:55.410 --> 00:39:58.680
the alert can be either
a ticket or a page that

00:39:58.680 --> 00:40:01.440
requires an immediate response.

00:40:01.440 --> 00:40:03.960
And it might be
version-specific,

00:40:03.960 --> 00:40:07.530
location-specific, or
even cluster-specific.

00:40:07.530 --> 00:40:11.610
And we can also get tickets
from our front support team

00:40:11.610 --> 00:40:14.680
if they're seeing
urgent customer issues.

00:40:14.680 --> 00:40:17.900
And I think we're
pretty lucky in GKE

00:40:17.900 --> 00:40:21.180
that so many Googlers are
contributors to the open source

00:40:21.180 --> 00:40:22.120
project.

00:40:22.120 --> 00:40:26.340
So when we do see issues
or bugs with an underlying

00:40:26.340 --> 00:40:29.160
version of Kubernetes,
we can pretty easily

00:40:29.160 --> 00:40:32.130
get those fixes in.

00:40:32.130 --> 00:40:36.330
So a common workflow might be
an on-caller receives an alert.

00:40:36.330 --> 00:40:39.120
We would then check our
dashboards and metrics

00:40:39.120 --> 00:40:41.880
to see the vital
signs of the cluster,

00:40:41.880 --> 00:40:45.270
look in the playbook for
mitigation and debugging tips.

00:40:45.270 --> 00:40:47.460
These might include
some of the ET CD bugs

00:40:47.460 --> 00:40:49.810
that I described earlier.

00:40:49.810 --> 00:40:54.540
And then we typically use one of
the following common procedures

00:40:54.540 --> 00:40:56.660
to mitigate against problems.

00:40:56.660 --> 00:40:58.900
So rolling back or
rolling forward.

00:40:58.900 --> 00:41:00.900
If it's a problem with
the GKE control plane,

00:41:00.900 --> 00:41:02.850
that's pretty
straightforward as we have

00:41:02.850 --> 00:41:04.950
full control of those jobs.

00:41:04.950 --> 00:41:09.390
If it's a problem with an
underlying Kubernetes version,

00:41:09.390 --> 00:41:11.010
it's a little bit trickier.

00:41:11.010 --> 00:41:15.090
We would need to cut a release
with a fix and upstream it.

00:41:15.090 --> 00:41:18.420
We can also upsize
or upgrade the master

00:41:18.420 --> 00:41:20.400
if it's a
cluster-specific issue.

00:41:20.400 --> 00:41:24.980
Typically, we'll try to give
the master a few more resources.

00:41:24.980 --> 00:41:26.670
And in the case of
higher workloads,

00:41:26.670 --> 00:41:29.130
that's pretty effective.

00:41:29.130 --> 00:41:31.930
Sometimes we'll have
to tweak the alert.

00:41:31.930 --> 00:41:35.220
If it's set too sensitively,
or if it fired in a way

00:41:35.220 --> 00:41:36.690
that didn't make sense.

00:41:36.690 --> 00:41:39.420
And this is part of iterating
on our alerts to make sure

00:41:39.420 --> 00:41:41.730
that they're all actionable.

00:41:41.730 --> 00:41:43.410
And finally, we
do sometimes have

00:41:43.410 --> 00:41:45.900
to resort to silencing alerts.

00:41:45.900 --> 00:41:48.190
We try to avoid this
as much as possible.

00:41:48.190 --> 00:41:51.570
But sometimes, it's
down to an issue

00:41:51.570 --> 00:41:54.640
that we can't take
direct action on.

00:41:54.640 --> 00:41:57.450
So for example, if we need to
wait for the next open source

00:41:57.450 --> 00:41:59.700
release, or if
it's an issue where

00:41:59.700 --> 00:42:02.010
the customer has
to go into the card

00:42:02.010 --> 00:42:05.800
console and fix the problem.

00:42:05.800 --> 00:42:08.370
So we've talked a little
bit about the routine alerts

00:42:08.370 --> 00:42:11.220
and routine mitigation actions.

00:42:11.220 --> 00:42:14.220
But sometimes, a large
outage occurs, something

00:42:14.220 --> 00:42:16.050
completely unexpected.

00:42:16.050 --> 00:42:18.960
And there's a large
impact instant.

00:42:18.960 --> 00:42:21.610
So what do we do
in those scenarios?

00:42:21.610 --> 00:42:25.260
So in SRE we follow a mantra
of coordinate, communicate,

00:42:25.260 --> 00:42:29.640
and control with an established
protocol in a crisis.

00:42:29.640 --> 00:42:31.560
So on-callers are
encouraged to pull

00:42:31.560 --> 00:42:36.280
in other members of their
team and get help and assign

00:42:36.280 --> 00:42:37.410
defined roles.

00:42:37.410 --> 00:42:39.840
So you have an
incident commander

00:42:39.840 --> 00:42:43.740
who is managing and coordinating
efforts, an operations

00:42:43.740 --> 00:42:46.600
lead and a communications lead.

00:42:46.600 --> 00:42:49.470
So there are established
communications channels

00:42:49.470 --> 00:42:53.670
that identify the impact
and notify our customers.

00:42:53.670 --> 00:42:57.720
You may have seen some of
these on our Cloud page.

00:42:57.720 --> 00:43:01.650
There's also escalation
pads to other parts of Cloud

00:43:01.650 --> 00:43:05.250
and also our developers who
have their own on-call rotations

00:43:05.250 --> 00:43:07.500
for various
subsystems within GKE

00:43:07.500 --> 00:43:10.860
and Kubernetes that they
have more expertise on.

00:43:10.860 --> 00:43:13.230
And after that instant,
it's really important

00:43:13.230 --> 00:43:15.300
that we write a post mortem.

00:43:15.300 --> 00:43:19.800
And even more importantly, we
learn from the action items

00:43:19.800 --> 00:43:22.560
from the post-mortem
and execute on them

00:43:22.560 --> 00:43:26.466
to make sure that the
instant is not repeated.

00:43:26.466 --> 00:43:27.840
And sometimes, we
even share some

00:43:27.840 --> 00:43:30.240
of these details
with our customers--

00:43:30.240 --> 00:43:35.370
so the timeline of the instant
the root cause and the action

00:43:35.370 --> 00:43:36.490
items that were taken.

00:43:39.710 --> 00:43:41.690
So that's what we're
doing on our side

00:43:41.690 --> 00:43:44.750
to improve the
resilience of the system.

00:43:44.750 --> 00:43:48.620
To wrap up, as potential
regional cluster users,

00:43:48.620 --> 00:43:53.540
what can you do to help improve
the resilience of your cluster

00:43:53.540 --> 00:43:56.700
and also help us
not to get paged?

00:43:56.700 --> 00:43:59.930
So in the open source
version of this talk

00:43:59.930 --> 00:44:04.760
that I gave at CubeCon,
we talk about ensuring

00:44:04.760 --> 00:44:07.520
that your ET CD backups
are done frequently

00:44:07.520 --> 00:44:11.060
and that you also test the
restore from backup procedure

00:44:11.060 --> 00:44:12.390
frequently as well.

00:44:12.390 --> 00:44:14.060
But as I mentioned
before, in GKE,

00:44:14.060 --> 00:44:16.700
this happens
automatically for you.

00:44:16.700 --> 00:44:19.190
And similarly, if you're
running your own master,

00:44:19.190 --> 00:44:22.100
I'd be advising that
you upgrade frequently

00:44:22.100 --> 00:44:25.340
and keep on top of the
latest security fixes.

00:44:25.340 --> 00:44:31.790
But again, this taken care for
you in our managed Kubernetes

00:44:31.790 --> 00:44:33.320
product.

00:44:33.320 --> 00:44:37.370
However as a GKE user, you
still have full control

00:44:37.370 --> 00:44:40.640
over the upgrade
cycle of your nodes.

00:44:40.640 --> 00:44:44.730
And we strongly recommend that
you enable node auto update

00:44:44.730 --> 00:44:48.380
so we can keep it in sync
with the master version,

00:44:48.380 --> 00:44:53.781
as well as node auto repair for
a fully self-healing cluster.

00:44:53.781 --> 00:44:56.030
JEFF JOHNSON: We also recommend
that your provisioning

00:44:56.030 --> 00:44:58.640
your project to have the
quota and capacities to handle

00:44:58.640 --> 00:45:01.080
these multi-zonal workloads.

00:45:01.080 --> 00:45:04.580
And something you can do to help
the Kubernetes scheduler out

00:45:04.580 --> 00:45:07.040
is to provide resource
limits for your pods

00:45:07.040 --> 00:45:12.200
and allow it to schedule those
a little bit more intelligently.

00:45:12.200 --> 00:45:14.150
As an application
developer, it's

00:45:14.150 --> 00:45:16.500
important that you're aware
of those high availability

00:45:16.500 --> 00:45:17.720
scenarios.

00:45:17.720 --> 00:45:19.970
High availability
masters, regional masters

00:45:19.970 --> 00:45:24.050
do not make your workloads high
availability out of the box.

00:45:24.050 --> 00:45:27.030
Spread your pods across
multiple zones in machines

00:45:27.030 --> 00:45:28.700
using pod anti-affinity.

00:45:28.700 --> 00:45:31.010
And ensure that you're using
regional storage and data

00:45:31.010 --> 00:45:31.510
services.

00:45:34.170 --> 00:45:36.210
So we thank you all for
joining us in this talk

00:45:36.210 --> 00:45:37.920
and learning about GKE.

00:45:37.920 --> 00:45:39.390
In the interest of
time, Tina and I

00:45:39.390 --> 00:45:40.890
are going to have to
give up this room.

00:45:40.890 --> 00:45:42.514
But we're going to
be available outside

00:45:42.514 --> 00:45:44.280
to answer any questions
you might have.

00:45:44.280 --> 00:45:46.290
And otherwise, please
enjoy the rest of the day.

00:45:46.290 --> 00:45:46.800
Thank you.

00:45:46.800 --> 00:45:49.850
[MUSIC PLAYING]

